7,-0.869992733001709,-8.702239938429557e-06,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-4.172316494077677e-06,-0.0969536155462265,-2.3841855067985307e-07,-3.576278118089249e-07,-0.028171049430966377,-6.139089964563027e-05,-1.7762025890988298e-05,-1.5497195136049413e-06,-0.015524609945714474,-2.3483953555114567e-05,-2.3841855067985307e-07,0.0,-1.1920928244535389e-07,0.0,-2.0265558760002023e-06,-2.3841855067985307e-07,0.0,-1.1086402082582936e-05,-0.00773222278803587,-0.08200238645076752,0.0,0.0,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-1.1920928244535389e-07,0.0,-8.344646857949556e-07,-2.3841855067985307e-07,-2.50339189733495e-06,-0.006106528919190168,-1.1920858014491387e-05,-0.00015186110977083445,-0.23131747543811798,-4.0531076592742465e-06,-5.960462772236497e-07,-2.0265558760002023e-06,-0.011203357949852943,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-3.576278118089249e-07,-3.576278118089249e-07,-0.0016940779751166701,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-2.3841855067985307e-07,-0.0004576589271891862,-2.861018856492592e-06,-7.033323527139146e-06,-3.2186455882765586e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,32,6172,57960,10374,13233,90,44,31716,4990,57960,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,17,24984,481,16,1124,408,90,5187,2555,31716,311,57960,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,24,24984,220,18,1124,408,90,5187,2555,2137,3,323,57960,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,16,24984,481,18,1124,408,90,5187,2555,31716,311,57960,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,22,24984,481,16,1124,408,90,5187,2555,92,2418,220,7379,279,2168,315,279,1555,400,88,284,220,17,87,488,220,16,3,1212,57960,10374,13233,90,44,92,2418,220,17399,697,4226,304,279,1352,5201,88,284,14619,488,293,3,3263,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nA matrix $\\mathbf{M}$ takes $\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix},$ and $\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}.$  Find the image of the line $y = 2x + 1$ under $\\mathbf{M}.$  Express your answer in the form \"$y = mx + b$\".","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"121135392e01aa03af7cf7c3784890ae79183a4897d6b6ca20c0f6a913d6a50a"},"seconds":76.03559041023254,"seed":278370643423467110,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we need to find the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) that transforms the given vectors and then use this matrix to determine the image of the line \\(y = 2x + 1\\).\n\nStep 1: Determine the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nWe can express the given transformations as:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} \\implies \\begin{cases} 2a - b = 9 \\\\ 2c - d = 3 \\end{cases}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} \\implies \\begin{cases} a - 3b = 7 \\\\ c - 3d = -1 \\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nWe now have the system of equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n2a - b = 9 \\\\\na - 3b = 7\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nSolving for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n\\[\n2a - b = 9 \\quad \\text{(1)}\n\\]\n\\[\na - 3b = 7 \\quad \\text{(2)}\n\\]\n\nMultiply equation (2) by 2:\n\\[\n2a - 6b = 14 \\quad \\text{(3)}\n\\]\n\nSubtract equation (1) from equation (3):\n\\[\n(2a - 6b) - (2a - b) = 14 - 9\n\\]\n\\[\n-5b = 5 \\implies b = -1\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\(b = -1\\) back into equation (1):\n\\[\n2a - (-1) = 9 \\implies 2a + 1 = 9 \\implies 2a = 8 \\implies a = 4\n\\]\n\nNext, solve for \\(c\\) and \\(d\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n2c - d = 3 \\\\\nc - 3d = -1\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nMultiply equation (4) by 2:\n\\[\n2c - 6d = -2 \\quad \\text{(5)}\n\\]\n\nSubtract equation (5) from equation (4):\n\\[\n(2c - d) - (2c - 6d) = 3 - (-2)\n\\]\n\\[\n5d = 5 \\implies d = 1\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\(d = 1\\) back into equation (4):\n\\[\n2c - 1 = 3 \\implies 2c = 4 \\implies c = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nStep 2: Find the image of the line \\(y = 2x + 1\\).\n\nThe line \\(y = 2x + 1\\) can be parameterized by \\(\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ 2x + 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nApply \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) to this parameterization:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ 2x + 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ 2x + 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4x - (2x + 1) \\\\ 2x + (2x + 1) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2x - 1 \\\\ 4x + 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nLet the new coordinates be \\((x', y')\\):\n\\[\nx' = 2x - 1\n\\]\n\\[\ny' = 4x + 1\n\\]\n\nExpress \\(x\\) in terms of \\(x'\\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{x' + 1}{2}\n\\]\n\nSubstitute this into the expression for \\(y'\\):\n\\[\ny' = 4 \\left(\\frac{x' + 1}{2}\\right) + 1 = 2(x' + 1) + 1 = 2x' + 3\n\\]\n\nThus, the image of the line \\(y = 2x + 1\\) under \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{y = 2x + 3}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{y = 2x + 3}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
